417] [17592186531545 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531545 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531545 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eec87c06-1424-53ca-8b89-733ac02a4134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531592 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531592 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531592 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531592 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=5$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531592 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "551dfc8f-f76b-5f91-a118-1f244aa7dd9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531592 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=5\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $5,$ while $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c35b6843ace65d2261f0910fbeae0e64383e7e325e3b851652b3aa421e311094.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531638 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531638 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531638 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531638 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/aa/aa9c640445da2c47bfabe3afe2cacb2acd2e67e42e052260c1cdf87d5c81dd16.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531638 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3cbf898-5adf-5f1a-9995-178ecdb7db19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531638 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a plane parallel to the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $2,$ we must have $z=2$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
